The Ardia Linux host draws the whole window itself: its sidebar and page card sit on the BrowserView, and it hides the
caption buttons. Where the compositor decorates a window itself (it answered that toplevel's xdg-decoration with
server-side, as Hyprland does, or there is no answer and the desktop is a tiling one), Chromium's custom Linux frame
only adds a themed band with its own shadow and rounded corners inside the compositor's. The host names those windows
through g_ardia_linux_borderless_frame; for them the frame view and its layout report no border, input or shadow
insets and no corner radius, and paint no frame, so the client area is the whole window. A thin band along the edges
still answers as a resize border so a floating window can be resized where the compositor draws none; a tiled one
has none. The hook is null outside the Linux host. Each XdgToplevel keeps the mode its compositor last answered,
WaylandToplevelExtension::CompositorDecorationMode reads it for a window, and
g_ardia_linux_compositor_decoration_changed tells the host which window's answer changed.
Chrome maps its startup window before the host starts, so X11 sets _GTK_FRAME_EXTENTS from the frame insets it had
then and rewrites it only on a window state change. When the host lays the frame out again (FrameTypeChanged), the
browser's window tree host now asks X11 for the insets once more through X11Extension::RefreshDecorationInsets, so
a window manager that honours the property sees none.
WindowSizerLinux remembers which browsers it gave Chromium's default size, and ArdiaLinuxTakeDefaultSizedBrowser hands
that to the host once per browser, so the host gives a borderless window the frame margin back only when its bounds
came from the default size, not from saved or copied bounds that happen to match it.
